[{"id":"bram-report-lutram","itemId":"block-ram","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">ram64</code> is a memory of 64 bytes that is read with no clock edge: <code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code> is always the entry at <code class=\"nobreak\">addr</code>. Below is the Slice Logic table of its Utilization report. Slice LUTs counts every LUT the design uses, and the rows under it say what each does: logic, or memory.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the LUT as Memory row: eight. A LUT6 used as memory holds 64 bits, one for each of 64 addresses, so one LUT holds one bit of every byte, and a byte has eight bits. LUT as Logic is 0: reading needs nothing beyond the memory LUTs themselves.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/ram64/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":32,"excerpt":["+----------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","|          Site Type         | Used | Fixed | Prohibited | Available | Util% |","+----------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","| Slice LUTs                 |    8 |     0 |          0 |     63400 |  0.01 |","|   LUT as Logic             |    0 |     0 |          0 |     63400 |  0.00 |","|   LUT as Memory            |    8 |     0 |          0 |     19000 |  0.04 |","|     LUT as Distributed RAM |    8 |     0 |            |           |       |","|     LUT as Shift Register  |    0 |     0 |            |           |       |","| Slice Registers            |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Flip Flop    |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Latch        |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","| F7 Muxes                   |    0 |     0 |          0 |     31700 |  0.00 |","| F8 Muxes                   |    0 |     0 |          0 |     15850 |  0.00 |","| Unique Control Sets        |    1 |       |          0 |     15850 | <0.01 |","+----------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+"],"ask":"How many LUTs does ram64 use as memory?","answer":"8","numeric":true},{"id":"bram-predict-read","itemId":"block-ram","role":"core","promptHtml":"This is lesson 6.3's <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code>, an array of 1,024 bytes written and read at the rising edge. When the window opens, entry 8 holds 11 and entry 9 holds 10, in hexadecimal; <code class=\"nobreak\">addr</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code> are drawn in hexadecimal too. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">dout[0]</code>, bit 0 of <code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code>, for every cycle.","explanationHtml":"<p><code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code> takes the entry <code class=\"nobreak\">addr</code> pointed at just before each edge, as it was before any write at that edge. 11 ends in 1 and 10 in 0, so reading 8, 9, 8 gives 1, 0, 1. At the edge that starts cycle 3, <code class=\"nobreak\">we</code> writes 01 into entry 9 while the same edge reads it, so <code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code> gets the old 10: 0. The next read of entry 9 gets 01, so 1, and entry 8 still gives 1.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"ram-read","source":"hdl/level-06/ram1k/ram1k_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"cycle","cycles":6,"signals":[{"label":"clk","path":"ram1k_tb.clk","width":1,"role":"clock","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[1,"1"],[1.5,"0"],[2,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[4,"1"],[4.5,"0"],[5,"1"],[5.5,"0"]]},{"label":"we","path":"ram1k_tb.we","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[2.5,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[5.5,"1"]]},{"label":"addr[9:0]","path":"ram1k_tb.addr","width":10,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0000001000"],[0.5,"0000001001"],[1.5,"0000001000"],[2.5,"0000001001"],[4.5,"0000001000"],[5.5,"0000000000"]]},{"label":"din[7:0]","path":"ram1k_tb.din","width":8,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"00000000"],[2.5,"00000001"],[3.5,"00000000"]]},{"label":"dout[0]","path":"ram1k_tb.dout0","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"1"],[1,"0"],[2,"1"],[3,"0"],[4,"1"]],"samples":["1","0","1","0","1","1"]}]},"answer":"dout[0]"},{"id":"bram-find-bug","itemId":"block-ram","role":"stretch","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k_bug</code> should behave like lesson 6.3's <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code>: <code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code> should follow a new <code class=\"nobreak\">addr</code> one cycle later, so that Vivado can build it from block RAM. Its testbench, the same as <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code>'s, fails. Pick the line that is wrong.","explanationHtml":"<p><code>assign dout = mem[addr];</code> reads the array with no clock edge, so <code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code> follows <code class=\"nobreak\">addr</code> at once and the testbench sees each entry a cycle early. That is also why Vivado cannot use block RAM, which reads only at an edge: it builds this memory from LUTs. Reading at the edge, <code>dout &lt;= mem[addr];</code> in an <code class=\"nobreak\">always_ff</code> block, gives <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code>'s timing and its RAMB18E1.</p>","type":"find-bug","file":"hdl/level-06/ram1k_bug/ram1k_bug.sv","firstLine":1,"lines":[[{"text":"// A memory of 1,024 bytes. At a rising edge where we is 1, the entry at","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// addr takes din. dout should be the entry at addr as it was just before","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// each rising edge, so that it follows a new addr one cycle later.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"ram1k_bug","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" (","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":"       clk,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":"       we,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"9","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] addr,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"7","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] din,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"output","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"7","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] dout","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":");","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"7","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] mem [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"1023","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"];","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"always_ff","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"@","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"(","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"posedge","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" clk)","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"    ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"if","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" (we) mem[addr] ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"<=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" din;","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"assign","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" dout ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" mem[addr];","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"endmodule","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}]],"bugLine":16,"fixLine":"always_ff @(posedge clk) dout <= mem[addr];"},{"id":"bram-report-tile","itemId":"block-ram","role":"core","promptHtml":"Vivado built lesson 6.3's <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code>, 1,024 bytes, from one RAMB18E1. Below is the Memory table of its Utilization report. A block RAM tile is one 36-kilobit block RAM, which can also work as two 18-kilobit ones; the rows under it count each kind.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the Block RAM Tile row: 0.5. 1,024 bytes are 8,192 bits, which fit in one 18-kilobit RAMB18E1, half a tile; the other half is free for another memory. The RAMB18 row counts that one block, out of the chip's 270.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/ram1k/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":99,"excerpt":["+-------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","|     Site Type     | Used | Fixed | Prohibited | Available | Util% |","+-------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","| Block RAM Tile    |  0.5 |     0 |          0 |       135 |  0.37 |","|   RAMB36/FIFO*    |    0 |     0 |          0 |       135 |  0.00 |","|   RAMB18          |    1 |     0 |          0 |       270 |  0.37 |","|     RAMB18E1 only |    1 |       |            |           |       |","+-------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+"],"ask":"How many block RAM tiles does ram1k use?","answer":"0.5","numeric":true},{"id":"bram-report-bug","itemId":"block-ram","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k_bug</code> holds 1,024 bytes, like lesson 6.3's <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code>, but reads them with no clock edge, so Vivado could not use block RAM. Below is the Slice Logic table of its Utilization report.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the LUT as Memory row: 128. 1,024 bytes are 8,192 bits, and a LUT6 used as memory holds 64 of them, so the memory takes 8,192 ÷ 64 = 128 LUTs, where <code class=\"nobreak\">ram1k</code> took half a block RAM tile. The F7 and F8 Muxes rows are there because each bit of <code class=\"nobreak\">dout</code> must be picked from 16 of those LUTs.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/ram1k_bug/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":32,"excerpt":["+----------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","|          Site Type         | Used | Fixed | Prohibited | Available | Util% |","+----------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","| Slice LUTs                 |  140 |     0 |          0 |     63400 |  0.22 |","|   LUT as Logic             |   12 |     0 |          0 |     63400 |  0.02 |","|   LUT as Memory            |  128 |     0 |          0 |     19000 |  0.67 |","|     LUT as Distributed RAM |  128 |     0 |            |           |       |","|     LUT as Shift Register  |    0 |     0 |            |           |       |","| Slice Registers            |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Flip Flop    |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Latch        |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","| F7 Muxes                   |   64 |     0 |          0 |     31700 |  0.20 |","| F8 Muxes                   |   32 |     0 |          0 |     15850 |  0.20 |","| Unique Control Sets        |    4 |       |          0 |     15850 |  0.03 |","+----------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+"],"ask":"How many LUTs does ram1k_bug use as memory?","answer":"128","numeric":true}]